"c1.pergunta": "Where did Lula grow most: where he was already strong or where he was weak?",
"c1.curto": {"t": "Where Lula grew most", "rotulo": 30},
"c1.resposta": "Where he was weaker. Comparing municipalities in the same state, the larger Lula's first-round share, the less it rose: for every 10 points more, {pts} less. The same holds for Flávio: each candidate grew more where he was weaker.",
"c1.hipotese": "Each candidate gained more share of the two-candidate vote (or lost less) where he was weaker in the first round.",
"c1.fig1.titulo": "Each candidate grew more where he had a smaller first-round share",
"c1.fig1.sub": "Change in Lula's share of the two-candidate vote from the first round to the runoff (vertical) by the share he had in the first round (horizontal), in the {mun} municipalities. Each circle is a municipality, sized by its registered voters; the color shows whether Lula's share rose (red) or fell (blue), not who won the municipality. On the left are the municipalities where Flávio's first-round share was larger.",
"c1.fig1.aria": "Scatter plot of the {mun} municipalities: Lula's first-round share on the horizontal axis and the change in his share on the vertical axis. The trend slopes down from left to right: where Lula's first-round share was already high, it rose less.",
"c1.dica.fatia1": {"t": "Lula's share, 1st round", "rotulo": 30},
"c1.fig1.eixo_x": {"t": "Lula's share in the 1st round (Flávio's is 100% minus Lula's)", "rotulo": 64},
"c1.fig1.rot_acima": {"t": "above zero, Lula's share rose", "rotulo": 40},
"c1.fig1.rot_acima_curto": {"t": "above: Lula's rose", "rotulo": 32},
"c1.fig1.rot_abaixo": {"t": "below, Flávio's rose", "rotulo": 40},
"c1.fig1.rot_abaixo_curto": {"t": "below: Flávio's", "rotulo": 32},
// the annotation inside the chart, one line per item (desktop and phone)
"c1.fig1.anotacao": ["Where Lula's share was high,", "it rose less"],
"c1.fig1.anotacao_cel": ["Where Lula's", "share was high,", "it rose less"],
"c1.fig1.como1": "**Lula's share** = Lula's votes ÷ (Lula's votes + Flávio's votes). The change is the runoff share minus the first-round share, in percentage points (pp). Flávio's is the same, with the sign reversed.",
"c1.fig1.como2": "Example, Brazil: {s1} in the first round and {s2} in the runoff, {ds}",
"c1.fig1.como3": "The line is the trend with all municipalities pooled, weighted by registered voters: {coef} point per 10. The deciding test compares municipalities in the same state and is under “See details”.",
"c1.fig1.como4": "Limit: an association between municipalities; it does not say how each voter voted.",
"c1.lado.tit": "How to read it",
"c1.lado.1": "This direction was expected even with no change in behavior: where a candidate already had a lot, there is less room to grow. What matters is the size of the drop, which is under “See details”.",
"c1.lado.2": "The association is between municipalities and does not say how each voter voted.",
"c1.chegou.texto": "Lula's share rose more where it was smaller in the first round. Stated for both: each candidate gained more share (or lost less) where he was weaker in the first round.",
"c1.chegou.porque": "**Why this direction: **in a simple model, in which the net vote gains are split in the same proportion everywhere, the share rises less where the candidate was already strong. In Brazil, {cap} of the votes the two gained went to Lula (his share of the combined gain{nota}), above his first-round share ({s1}). Had the larger part of the combined gain gone to Flávio, the same arithmetic would predict the opposite, and the expected hypothesis would be the one on his side.",
"c1.chegou.variavel": "by the share he had in the first round",
"c1.texto.1": "Comparing municipalities in the same state, for every 10 more points of Lula's first-round share, his share rose {pts} less from the first round to the runoff ({ic0} to {ic1}). Between a municipality in the bottom quartile and one in the top, the difference is {iq} point. For Brazil as a whole, the direction is the same ({br} point per 10).",
"c1.texto.2": "**In votes per voter: **Flávio gained more votes where he was already stronger ({cf} point of the electorate more for every 10 points of his first-round share, {cf0} to {cf1}); Lula's gain in votes does not track the share he had ({cl0} to {cl1}).",
"c1.texto.3": "**Limit: **this direction is expected even if the vote gains were split the same way everywhere; the association is between municipalities and does not say how each voter voted.",
"c1.texto.4": "**Against an equal split (exploratory, not recorded before the calculations; calculated after the statistical review): **if the net gain of the two were split in every municipality as in Brazil as a whole ({cap} to Lula, excluding voters abroad, who are left out of the between-municipality calculations), the same calculation would give {esp} point per 10 ({esp0} to {esp1}); the observed value is {obs}. The share fell about half as much as an equal split would give: where each candidate was already stronger, he took a larger part of the net gain than in Brazil as a whole. This applies equally to both, because it is the same calculation. Part of the drop may be regression to the mean (Barnett, van der Pols and Dobson, 2005): when the first-round share has a transient component, the slope comes out more negative than it really is; with complete counts and weighting by registered voters, this effect should be small, and it would only widen the difference.",
"c1.funil.secao": "How much a municipality varies just because it is small",
"c1.funil.titulo": "Small municipalities vary more just because they are small",
"c1.funil.sub": "Change in Lula's share (vertical) by the municipality's registered voters (horizontal), with the band the model expects for each size.",
"c1.funil.texto": "{n} municipalities fall outside the {p998} band: in {acima}, the change in Lula's share was above the band; in {abaixo}, Flávio's.",
"c1.rank.secao": "Where Lula's share rose most",
