"c4.fig2.eixo": {"t": "of every 100 votes in the net change in turnout", "rotulo": 52},
"c4.fig2.nao_deu": "It could not be estimated with confidence in {n} states: ",
"c4.fig2.nao_deu_lista": "{lista}.",
"c4.fig2.fonte": "Source: TSE, results by polling station; ecological regression comparing polling stations in the same city, by region and state; our calculations.",
"c4.fig2.como1": "**The same ecological regression** as in chapter 3, but comparing polling stations in the same city ({sec} polling stations), with a municipality effect: whatever moved the whole city stays out of the calculation, and only the differences between polling stations in the same city count. Run separately by region and by state. Bootstrap interval: in Brazil and the regions, whole states resampled; in the states, whole municipalities, with all their polling stations. That is why “out of every 100 additional votes cast” holds when comparing polling stations in the same city. Part of the change is common to the whole city and does not track any of the three sources: in Brazil, {comum} votes, on net, for Lula, or {pct} of the additional runoff votes. It stays out of the split.",
"c4.fig2.como2": "**Precision criterion**, the same for both: at least 30 municipalities; an estimate between 0% and 100% (outside that range, the model does not describe the place, and all its sources get the † mark); and a half-width of the 95% confidence interval of at most 10 points. Where it fails, the reason is shown.",
"c4.fig2.como3": "“Most went to X” only when the whole interval lies above 50% for X.",
"c4.fig2.como4": "**Robustness checks**, recorded beforehand, which do not change the reading: without the polling stations whose eligible voters change by more than 5 between rounds (note {n22}), without those that received merged polling stations, unweighted, and with a different interval draw. With all of them, the reading holds in {n} of the {total} places, Brazil among them. Without the municipality effect, the polling stations repeat the across-municipality calculation, including the estimates outside 0% to 100%: what changes the answer is comparing polling stations in the same city. The annulled votes for candidate 28 count as null (note {n21}).",
"c4.fig2.como5": "**The across-municipality calculation**, which this figure showed before, is in the “See details” table, alongside. It gave most of the net change to one of the two in {muda}; comparing polling stations, these states have no clear side.",
"c4.lado.tit": "Two layers",
"c4.lado.1": "The first is a count: where abstention rose and fell, with no margin of error. The second is an estimate: where the net change in turnout went, shown only where it meets the precision criterion set beforehand.",
"c4.lado.2": "A municipality's electorate changes between rounds, and part of the change in turnout may come from voters who voted somewhere else in the other round.",
"c4.chegou.texto": "Lula's share rose more (and Flávio's fell more) where abstention fell more.",
"c4.chegou.porque": "**Why this direction: **by the same simple model as in chapter 1: if the part of vote gains that goes to a candidate (in Brazil, {cap} for Lula) is larger than his share, his share rises more where there are more vote gains, such as those from people who started turning out. If Flávio had taken the larger share of the combined gain, the prediction would be the opposite.",
"c4.chegou.variavel": "by the change in the abstention rate",
"c4.texto.1": "The change in Lula's share does not clearly track the change in abstention: the interval includes zero in every version (within states, from {ic0} to {ic1} points of share per point of abstention).",
"c4.texto.2": "**In votes: **both gained more where abstention fell more. For every point less of abstention, Lula gained {cl} points of the electorate more (from {cl0} to {cl1}) and Flávio, {cf} (from {cf0} to {cf1}), comparing municipalities in the same state.",
"c4.texto.3": "**Limit: **a municipality's electorate changes between rounds{n05}, and part of the change in turnout may come from voters who voted somewhere else in the other round.",
