"c5.pergunta": "Is the vote for deputy and senator linked to the change between the rounds?",
"c5.curto": {"t": "Deputies and senators", "rotulo": 30},
"c5.resposta": "No clear link appeared, on Lula's side or on Flávio's. Where voters already voted more for left-wing parties for federal deputy, Lula did not clearly grow more than in municipalities in the same state with the same first-round vote for him; where they voted more for right-wing parties, the same holds for Flávio. If there is a link, it is small. The same holds for state deputy and senator.",
"c5.resposta.ic": "{a} and {b} points",
"c5.hipotese": "Where voters already voted for left-bloc parties for deputy or senator, but not for Lula for president in the first round, Lula grew more in the runoff.",
"c5.status_inc": "Inconclusive for all three offices",
"c5.status_cargo": "Result by office",
"c5.dica.titulo": "{cargo}: {versao}",
"c5.dica.coef10": {"t": "Coefficient per 10 points", "rotulo": 30},
"c5.fig1.titulo": "For all three offices, every interval crosses zero",
"c5.fig1.titulo_muda": "The result changes from one office to another",
"c5.fig1.sub": "Association between the potential advantage and the change in Lula's share of the two-candidate vote, by office: effect of 10 points of advantage, with 95% interval.",
"c5.fig1.leitura": "Each line is a version of the test: the dot is the estimate and the bar, the 95% interval. All intervals cross zero and are narrow: if there is an association, it is small. In Flávio's mirror test, the same question is turned toward the right bloc.",
"c5.fig1.aria": "Estimates and 95% intervals of the split-ticket voting test, by office",
"c5.fig1.eixo": {"t": "effect of 10 points of advantage, in points of Lula's share", "rotulo": 60},
"c5.fig1.esq": {"t": "← opposite of predicted", "rotulo": 26},
"c5.fig1.como1": "**Left-bloc %** = votes for left-bloc parties for the office ÷ valid votes for the office. The bloc comes from the expert survey (2022 wave): parties positioned at up to {corte} on the 0-to-10 scale. No party falls between {v0} and {v1}, and any cutoff in that range gives the same classification: the cutoff does not depend on a fine choice.",
"c5.fig1.como2": "**Potential advantage** = left-bloc % − Lula's share of valid votes in the first round, by municipality.",
"c5.fig1.como3": "Test: the change in Lula's share explained by the potential advantage, with Lula's first-round share as a control, comparing municipalities in the same state, weighted by registered voters and with standard errors clustered by state.",
"c5.fig1.como4": "Flávio's mirror test: right-bloc % − Flávio's share of valid votes, explaining the change in his share.",
"c5.fig1.fonte": "Source: TSE, results by municipality and votes by party; Bolognesi, Ribeiro, Codato and Silva (2025); our calculations.",
"c5.lado.tit": "Limit",
"c5.lado.1": "An association across municipalities: it does not say whether the voters who voted for a bloc party for deputy were the ones who changed their vote for president.",
"c5.lado.2": "For senator, {ufs} are left out, where the left bloc got less than 5% of the votes (a lack of candidates, not of votes).",
"c5.chegou.texto": "where voters already voted for left-bloc parties for deputy or senator, but not for Lula for president in the first round, Lula grew more in the runoff.",
"c5.chegou.teste": "**The main test, **for each office: the change in Lula's share explained by the potential advantage, with Lula's first-round share of valid votes as a control, comparing municipalities in the same state. The candidates for deputy and senator are the same throughout the state, and comparing municipalities in the same state separates the voters' choice from the supply of candidates. Predicted: a positive coefficient. Federal deputy is the main office, set beforehand: it exists throughout the country, including the Federal District, and has one vote per voter; there is no correction across the three offices, and, had they disagreed, federal deputy would have decided.",
"c5.chegou.cargo": "{cargo}{coef} points per 10 points of advantage (95% interval: {ic}); wild cluster bootstrap p-value: {p}. {porque} Classification: {classe}; {conf}.",
"c5.chegou.unica": "**The only version that moves away from zero **is the one that also controls for the other candidates' votes, for federal and state deputy: a sign opposite to the prediction and a small effect. With Lula's share of valid votes as a control, the coefficient on the potential advantage is the same as on the left-bloc %: the calculation measures the bloc vote across municipalities with the same Lula share. With the other candidates' votes also controlled for, what remains is small and negative. The robustness checks do not change the classification.",
"c5.chegou.todas": "All versions, {cargo}",
"c5.chegou.tabela": "{cargo}: all versions (coefficient per 10 points of advantage; continuous scale per 1 point of the scale)",
"c5.texto.1": "Comparing municipalities in the same state with the same first-round Lula share, the vote for left-bloc parties for federal deputy does not appear associated with the change in Lula's share: for every 10 points of potential advantage, the change is between {f0} and {f1} points. The interval is narrow around zero, and the same holds for state deputy ({e0} to {e1}) and senator ({s0} to {s1}), for the continuous scale, for the 2018 scale, for the rules on Missão and on sub judice votes, and for Flávio's mirror version, with the right bloc.",
"c5.ressalvas.secao": "Caveats",
"c5.ressalvas.n12": "note N12",
"c5.ressalvas": "Sub judice votes: excluded from valid votes, as in the official result; two robustness checks with and without them give the same result{n02}. Partido Missão, with no position on the scale: its votes are within valid votes and outside the left bloc; the check with its votes excluded gives the same result{n11}. Parties on which the experts agree less: {n12}.",

