"c9.fig4.titulo": "Just over half of voters aged 70 or over turned out in the first round; from 80 on, most stayed away",
"c9.fig4.sub": "Share of registered voters of each age who turned out in the first round, in Brazil. The line marks half.",
"c9.fig4.leitura": "It is a count of people, not an estimate. The register still includes voters who have died and were not removed, more so at older ages; so the share who turned out at those ages is lower than the real one.",
"c9.fig4.eixo": {"t": "turned out, in %", "rotulo": 30},
"c9.fig4.ref": {"t": "half", "rotulo": 12},
"c9.fig4.aria": "Bars: share of registered voters of each age who turned out in the first round, in Brazil",
"c9.fig4.dica.taxa": {"t": "Turned out", "rotulo": 22},
"c9.fig4.dica.aptos": {"t": "Registered voters", "rotulo": 22},
"c9.fig4.dica.comp": {"t": "Voted", "rotulo": 22},
"c9.fig4.fonte": "Source: TSE, turnout and abstention profile, first round (data of 13/10/2026); own calculation.",
"c9.fig4.como1": "The calculation: in each age band of the register, the number who voted divided by registered voters, adding up the states; voters abroad are kept apart.",
"c9.fig4.como2": "The TSE has not published the runoff by age, neither in its open data nor in its statistics dashboard (checked on 28/10/2026).",
"c9.r2.script": "Record: the five questions of the second round of questions, with the test and what would count as a finding, before the calculations. Calculation: scripts/perfil_idade.py, which writes dados/perfil_idade.json.",
"c9.fig5.titulo": "After 70, women turned out less than men; before 60, more",
"c9.fig5.sub": "Share of registered voters who turned out in the first round, by age, women and men, in Brazil.",
"c9.fig5.leitura": "It is a count of people, by the gender in the register. It shows who turned out, not whom they voted for.",
"c9.fig5.aria": "Dots: share of women and of men who turned out in the first round, by age, in Brazil",
"c9.fig6.titulo": "In polling stations with more older registered voters, abstention fell slightly more and Lula's share rose slightly less",
"c9.fig6.sub": "Change from the first round to the runoff, in points, per 10 points more voters aged 60 or over among those registered at the polling station, comparing polling stations in the same city. The mark is the 97.5% interval.",
"c9.fig6.leitura": "Left of zero, the change was smaller in polling stations with more older registered voters: abstention fell more, and Lula's share rose less (Flávio's fell less). It holds for polling stations, not for people.",
"c9.fig6.eixo": {"t": "change per 10 points of older voters, in p.p.", "rotulo": 56},
"c9.fig6.grupo_a": "Change in abstention",
"c9.fig6.grupo_s": "Change in Lula's share of the two-candidate vote",
"c9.fig6.aria": "Coefficients with interval: change in abstention and in Lula's share per 10 points more registered voters aged 60 or over, in Brazil and in the regions",
"c9.fig6.fonte": "Source: TSE, voter profile by polling station (register of July 2026) and results by polling station for both rounds ({sec} polling stations); own calculation.",
"c9.fig6.como1": "The calculation: in each polling station, the share of registered voters aged 60 or over against the change in abstention and in Lula's share of the two-candidate vote, with a municipality effect (only polling stations in the same city are compared) and weighting by first-round registered voters. For Flávio, it is the same line read from the other side.",
"c9.fig6.como2": "Interval: bootstrap with {n} replications, drawing whole states; with two measures, the interval is 97.5%. The robustness checks are in See details: all with the interval below zero.",
"leg.ic975": {"t": "97.5% interval", "rotulo": 22},
"tab.ic975": {"t": "97.5% CI, bootstrap", "rotulo": 28},
"c9.det.i1": "**The calculation: **the TSE publishes, by age, how many registered voters voted and how many stayed away. Here, the share who turned out in each age group, adding up the states, with voters abroad kept apart, and in the first round only: the runoff has not been published. It is a count of people, with no model. **Checks: **in Brazil including voters abroad, the profile has {pa} registered voters and {pc} who voted, against {oa} and {oc} in the official result. In each municipality the numbers do not match, because the profile counts the {tte} voters in transit in their home city; so nothing here is shown by municipality ({n24}). At the ages with voluntary voting (16, 17 and 70 or over), no one is in the compulsory-voting column; from 18 to 69, the {anal} with voluntary voting are the voters who cannot read or write, for whom voting is voluntary at any age.",
"c9.tab6.titulo": "Share of registered voters who turned out in the first round, by age group, in %",
"c9.tab6.nota": "16 and 17, and 70 or over: voluntary voting.",
"c9.det.i2": "**The calculation: **that of question 6, in the age bands of 70 or over, and the first band in which fewer than half turned out. **Result: **in Brazil, 80 to 84; already 75 to 79 in {lugares}.",
"c9.tab7.titulo": "Share of voters aged 70 or over who turned out in the first round, by age, in %",
"c9.tab7.primeira": {"t": "Fewer than half from", "rotulo": 30},
"c9.det.i3": "**The calculation: **among those who stayed away in the first round, the share of each age group, next to its share of registered voters. If everyone stayed away at the same rate, the two would be equal.",
"c9.tab8.titulo": "Share of each age group among those who stayed away in the first round and among registered voters, in %",
"c9.tab8.cel": "{b} of those who stayed away; {a} of registered voters",
"c9.det.i4": "**The calculation: **that of question 6, separating women and men by the gender in the register; {sem} registered voters without that information are left out.",
"c9.tab9.titulo": "Share of women and of men who turned out in the first round, by age, in %",
"c9.tab9.genero": {"t": "Gender", "rotulo": 14},
"c9.det.i5": "**The test: **in each polling station, the share of registered voters aged 60 or over, from the TSE voter profile by polling station (register of July 2026), against two changes from the first round to the runoff: in abstention and in Lula's share of the two-candidate vote. As in question 4, only polling stations in the same city are compared, weighting by first-round registered voters; bootstrap with {n} replications, drawing whole states; with two measures, the interval is 97.5%. **Would count as a finding: **for each measure, the interval for Brazil as a whole on one side of zero, with the same sign and on the same side in the six robustness checks. **Result: **a finding in both (figure {fig}). In abstention, {iqa} between the quarter of polling stations with the most registered voters aged 60 or over and the quarter with the fewest, at the limit of what the rule calls small (half a point); in Lula's share, {iqs}, small. Adding Lula's first-round share to the same calculation changes nothing: it is not the link from question 4 under another name. {sp} polling stations without a profile are left out.",
"c9.tab10.titulo": "Change per 10 points more registered voters aged 60 or over, polling stations in the same city: the calculation and the robustness checks",
"c9.tab10.a": {"t": "Abstention", "rotulo": 14},
"c9.tab10.s": {"t": "Lula's share", "rotulo": 16},
"c9.tab10.p70": "70 or over, instead of 60",
"c9.tab10.perfil_igual_aos_aptos": "Only polling stations where registered and eligible voters differ by under 5%",
"c9.tab10.sem_efeito_de_municipio": "Context: also comparing cities",
"c9.tab10.com_fatia_do_lula_no_1t": "Context: with Lula's first-round share",
"c9.limites.5": "Questions 6 to 9 count people, but only in the first round, and the register still includes voters who have died and were not removed, more so at older ages: the share who turned out at those ages is lower than the real one.",
"c9.limites.6": "Question 10 compares polling stations by the age of those registered there (register of July), not of those who voted.",

