"exp.disp.log": "{t} (log scale)",
"exp.disp.livre_leitura": "No trend line: the choice is free, and a line here would be a new calculation, outside the hypotheses recorded beforehand. Association between municipalities, not between people.",
"exp.disp.livre_como": "The variables are the same as in the chapters, by municipality: share, votes gained as % of first-round registered voters, abstention rate and votes for the other candidates as % of valid votes; plus the 7 municipal characteristics from chapter 8 (IBGE, 2022 Census and 2023 GDP), which do not exist for Boa Esperança do Norte (MT), left out of the chart when one of them is on an axis. The scale of each axis leaves out the most extreme {pct} on each side, which appears at the edge (with fewer than 400 municipalities in the selection, all of them fit), and includes zero when the variable is a change.",
"exp.funil.sub_fatia": "Change in Lula's share (vertical) against the municipality's registered voters (horizontal), with the band the model expects for each size. Outside the band, in red, the municipalities where Lula's share rose more than expected; in blue, those where Flávio's rose more than expected.",
"exp.funil.nota": "The band comes from the full set of {mun} municipalities and does not change with the selection; the selection only chooses which points appear.",
"exp.inc.de_a": "from {a} to {b}",
"exp.voto.tit": "Split-ticket voting: deputy and senator",
"exp.voto.aba_federal": {"t": "Federal deputy", "rotulo": 20},
"exp.voto.aba_estadual": {"t": "State deputy", "rotulo": 20},
"exp.voto.aba_senador": {"t": "Senator", "rotulo": 20},
"exp.voto.titulo_todas": "{cargo}: in all {total} versions, the interval crosses zero",
"exp.voto.titulo_algumas": "{cargo}: in {n} of the {total} versions, the interval crosses zero",
"exp.voto.sub": "Association between the potential advantage and the change in Lula's share, comparing municipalities in the same state: effect of 10 points of advantage, with the 95% interval, in each version of the test.",
"exp.voto.leitura": "Main test classification: {classe}. {conf} The version on the continuous scale uses a different unit and is in the table below.",
"exp.voto.conf_iguais": "The robustness checks give the same result.",
"exp.voto.conf_partes": "Robustness checks: {partes}.",
"exp.voto.aria": "Estimates and 95% intervals for the split-ticket voting test, {cargo}",
"exp.voto.fonte": "Source: TSE, results by municipality and votes by party; Bolognesi, Ribeiro, Codato and Silva (2025); our calculations.",
"exp.voto.como1": "**Potential advantage** = % of votes for left-bloc parties for the office − Lula's share of valid votes in the first round, by municipality. In Flávio's mirror version: right-bloc % − Flávio's share of valid votes.",
"exp.voto.como2": "Test: the change in Lula's share explained by the potential advantage, with Lula's first-round share as a control, comparing municipalities in the same state, weighted by registered voters and with standard errors clustered by state. Rule set beforehand: supported only if the 95% confidence interval lies entirely on the predicted side and the wild cluster bootstrap p-value is below {p}.",
"exp.voto.todas": "All versions, with the numbers",
"exp.voto.nota": "What each version changes, and the conclusion, are in {link}.",
"exp.inc.vieram_tit": "Where the votes came from",
"exp.inc.vieram_sub": "How each source of vote gains split, on net, between the two, in Brazil: estimate with the 95% interval. The other candidates' votes appear in four calculations, which do not agree.",
"exp.inc.vieram_leitura": "How the other candidates' votes split depends on the calculation. In the three that compare municipalities, some part falls outside 0% to 100%; comparing polling stations in the same city, none does, and the other candidates' votes split with no clear side. This applies to places, not people: everything is net, and some of those who voted for one of the other candidates in the first round may not have voted in the runoff, and vice versa.",
"exp.inc.vieram_como1": "**Ecological regression** (Goodman, 1953 and 1959): across municipalities, the votes each candidate gained are explained by the three sources of vote gains, all divided by registered voters and weighted by them; each candidate's part of each source adds up to 100%. With an intercept, part of the change falls outside the three sources. In the transition matrix (Romero et al., 2020), the first-round categories explain the runoff ones. In the polling-station calculation, the same three sources, comparing polling stations in the same city, with a municipality effect; the other two sources come from it.",
"exp.inc.vieram_como2": "Interval: bootstrap with {n} replications, resampling whole states. Only what meets the precision criterion is shown: estimate between 0% and 100% and interval half-width of up to 10 points.",
